   00590|     // expands each into a generated record, so computing trust rather than
   00591|     // assuming it arrives here as described data, on the same terms as every
   00592|     // vocabulary addition above.
   00593|     // The last forty-two arrive the same way, from issue #1075's
   00594|     // `data/seed/tool-resource-scopes.lino`: the words that let a tool say
   00595|     // *where* its effect lands rather than only what it does --
   00596|     // `remote_service`, `process_input`, `connector`, `namespaced`,
   00597|     // `substring`, `router`, `advertised`, `grounded`, `untouched` and the
   00598|     // prose around them. Every one is a `closure-generated-*.lino` record for a
   00599|     // token the new seed prose pulled into the total closure, not a
   00600|     // hand-written gap, which is the shape this floor exists to show.
   00601|     //
   00602|     // The last fifty-four arrive that way too, from issue #1085: the tokens
   00603|     // `data/seed/handler-rules.lino` and
   00604|     // `data/seed/multilingual-responses-policy.lino` introduced when eleven
   00605|     // handlers stopped being Rust -- the rule vocabulary (`agent_info`,
   00606|     // `backticks`, `cleaned`, `raw`, `literal`, `prompt`, `forms`), the
   00607|     // handler names themselves, and the response ids of their four-language
   00608|     // wording. Behaviour moving from Rust into seed data arrives as generated
   00609|     // closure records, which is what this floor is for. Four more followed when
   00610|     // those responses were given their Spanish text, which every supported
   00611|     // locale owes the others. Ten more arrived the same way with issues #1095
   00612|     // and #1099: two seed roles (`agentic_continuation_cue`,
   00613|     // `enumeration_cue`), the `agentic_continuation` handler and its
   00614|     // `continuation_cue` rule, that handler's five-language wording, and the
   00615|     // seeded notice `context_session_guessed_notice` from #1105 RC6. Every one
   00616|     // is behaviour that used to be Rust -- a hardcoded English phrase, an
   00617|     // exact-string comparison in the planner -- arriving as described data,
   00618|     // which is what this floor exists to record. Issue #1131 brought five more
